# Guest: blktests
# Joined with the guest's profile and that of what is under test, as in
# guest+blktests+block-layer. core/ikconfig.config builds the module
# that puts the kernel's .config at /proc/config.gz: a test asks it
# which options the kernel has, and is not run where it cannot. The
# guest loads the module at boot, as its blktests profile says
# (tasks/libguestfs/files/profiles). storage/block-test-devices.config
# builds null_blk and the loop devices, and storage/scsi-debug.config
# scsi_debug, which the block and scsi groups make their devices from.
# storage/dm-targets.config builds device mapper and its multipath
# target, for the dm group and for multipathd, which the guest's
# multipath-tools starts at boot and which fails with no dm_multipath.
# storage/md-raid.config builds the MD personalities of the md group,
# storage/srp-target.config the SRP initiator and target with the SCSI
# device handlers of the srp group, and fs/f2fs.config the file system
# a few block tests make.
# Three debug fragments are in it, though they slow every test: a few
# tests show their bug only as a report of KASAN (nvme/069, nvme/075),
# of DEBUG_VM (block/050, block/051) or of DEBUG_ATOMIC_SLEEP
# (nvme/055), and are not run on a kernel without. Under GCC, KASAN leaves Rust out, which Kconfig allows with
# it only under Clang: build with LLVM=1 to keep both.
core/ikconfig.config
storage/block-test-devices.config
storage/scsi-debug.config
storage/dm-targets.config
storage/md-raid.config
storage/srp-target.config
fs/f2fs.config
debug/kasan.config
debug/atomic-sleep.config
debug/debug-heavy.config
